        how: "This tree's own state table (render/aiPresenceOrbState.mjs's STATES) already covers 5 of thinking-orbs' 9 concepts under different names: listening=listening (exact), thinking is working/solving, responding is composing, idle is breathing, plus success/error which thinking-orbs has no equivalent of. Four are genuinely new: searching (a distinct retrieval/tool-call moment), connecting (waiting on a network/session), weaving (synthesising multiple sources), shaping (building/generating an artifact). Phase 2 of the same arc the ai-presence-orb-widget entry above closed: that entry's STATES table, its {speed,glow,depth,hueShift} multiplier shape and its entry-envelope mechanism (wake/swell/stutter) are the exact scaffolding a new state slots into -- no new architecture, four new STATES rows plus, if warranted, their own entry envelopes.",
        why: "Named OPEN rather than CLOSED-and-done or dismissed, because the blocking condition is not technical: this tree's own build discipline (read in the CLOSED entry above) refused to wire success/error until converseText() had a REAL observable outcome to dispatch, on the stated reasoning that an unwired state is decoration, not signal. The same test applies here. This engine's live AI loop (ui/sttLayer.js's converseText(), the one real /ai/chat consumer) is currently a flat listen -> think -> respond -> outcome cycle with no separately observable tool-call, retrieval, or multi-source-synthesis moment for searching/connecting/weaving/shaping to attach to. Adding the four rows today would be exactly the failure mode this tree's own orb work has repeatedly caught and refused to ship.",
        upstream: "Nothing to build yet. Waiting on the AI bridge (ai-bridge/) to expose a real, observable event for at least one of the four concepts -- a tool-call-started/finished pair is the most likely first candidate, matching converseText()'s existing engine:voiceTranscript/voiceReply/voiceError window-event pattern. When that arrives, wiring the state itself is small (see 'how'); until then this is a naming reservation, not a task with an obvious next action.\n\nBLOCKER RE-VERIFIED AT v4628 -- IT HOLDS, AND FOR A SHARPER REASON THAN STATED ABOVE. converseText() is still flat: ui/sttLayer.js dispatches exactly three events (engine:voiceTranscript at line 81, engine:voiceReply at 97, engine:voiceError at 102) and nothing between them. But the reasoning above weighed only /ai/chat, and there is a SECOND real AI consumer it did not: ai-bridge/ragBridge.js owns POST /ai/brain/ask and is genuinely two-stage -- retrieve (embed -> sqlite-vec ragSearch top-k) THEN generate (Ollama, grounded in the passages) -- returning sources, grounded: sources.length > 0, and answer: null with the note 'retrieval only (no LLM reachable)'. That is exactly the retrieval moment `searching` is named for and exactly the multi-source count `weaving` is named for. IT STILL UNBLOCKS NOTHING, and the reason is the precise one: its only browser consumer is ev.html:1563, a single awaited fetch, so the two stages collapse into ONE round-trip and the client never observes stage 1 finishing. There is nothing for `searching` to be lit DURING. So the blocking condition is not 'no two-stage AI work exists in this tree' -- it does -- but 'no two-stage AI work is separately observable from the page'. THE CONCRETE UNBLOCK IS THEREFORE SMALLER AND BETTER NAMED THAN 'waiting on the AI bridge': make /ai/brain/ask emit its two stages separately (stream, or a two-part response), at which point `searching` gets the retrieval window and `weaving` gets sources.length. `connecting` and `shaping` still have nothing to attach to and stay reserved.",
