        id: "draco-encode",
        state: "CLOSED",
        note: "DONE at v4601. tools/export/dracoEncode.mjs is the encoder tools/ship/dracoWeld-selfcheck.mjs's own v4163 closing note said this tree lacked (\"draco.js DECODES ONLY, and neither it nor glb-shrink ships an encoder we could vendor\") and tools/export/weldVertices.mjs's header had named as the unbuilt half of its own glb-shrink comparison (\"Draco-encode -- YES, and it is the big win: quantised integer positions are what voxel geometry IS\").\n\n*** THE FIRST BUILD ATTEMPT WAS THREE.JS'S OWN DRACOExporter.js, AND IT HUNG. *** examples/jsm/exporters/DRACOExporter.js (mrdoob/three.js r160, matching this tree's vendored three) is the natural sibling of the already-vendored decoder, so it was fetched and tried first, alongside its own examples/jsm/libs/draco/draco_encoder.js. That file's module-init promise never resolved: MEASURED at over 100 minutes of pinned CPU on a single 3-vertex encode before being killed. Diagnosed by instrumented step-logging in the actual page rather than guessed at: the hang is inside DracoEncoderModule() itself, before a single Draco API call runs. The file is asm.js-era Emscripten output (an `\"almost asm\"` directive, no `.wasm` file or `wasmBinary` reference anywhere, no `readyPromiseResolve`/`readyPromiseReject` pattern) -- an old build shape nobody had exercised in this sandbox's headless Chromium before. NOT vendored, and neither is DRACOExporter.js, since there was nothing working for it to call.\n\n*** draco3d (Google's OWN current npm package) WORKED ON THE FIRST TRY, IN PLAIN NODE, NO BROWSER AT ALL. *** Its Node-targeted build (draco_encoder_nodejs.js + a real, separate draco_encoder.wasm -- not asm.js) is what its own package.json test script runs; confirmed directly, a real triangle round-tripped to a 75-byte DRACO-magic buffer in milliseconds. vendor/draco-encoder/ carries these two files plus the Apache-2.0 LICENSE (fetched from google/draco's own repo -- the npm tarball carries no LICENSE file, only package.json's declaration) and a PROVENANCE.txt recording the rejected build so nobody re-tries it without reading why.\n\n*** A REAL BUG, FOUND BY THE ROUND-TRIP RATHER THAN BY READING THE SPEC. *** three.js's own DRACOExporter.js never captures what builder.AddFloatAttributeToMesh(...) returns -- it only writes a standalone .drc file, which does not need to. Embedding a Draco mesh inside a glTF file needs exactly that return value (Draco's own internal attribute id) for the KHR_draco_mesh_compression extension's own `attributes` map, so tools/export/dracoEncode.mjs captures it itself. The extension's map keys turned out to matter too, and got them wrong on the first pass: read directly off vendor/three/jsm/loaders/GLTFLoader.js's GLTFDracoMeshCompressionExtension, that map is keyed by glTF's SEMANTIC names (TEXCOORD_0, COLOR_0), not Draco's generic type names (TEX_COORD, COLOR) -- POSITION and NORMAL are spelled identically both ways, which is exactly what let a first draft's TEX_COORD key hide behind them: position and normal round-tripped correctly, the UV silently came back as all zeros. Caught by the round-trip test itself (decoded UVs did not match encoded ones), not by inspection; tools/ship/dracoEncode-selfcheck.mjs section 2 now pins the exact key spelling as a regression check.\n\n*** THE OTHER GAP dracoWeld-selfcheck.mjs LEFT OPEN -- \"an actual Draco-compressed GLB DECODED end to end\" -- IS ALSO CLOSED, and by the SAME round. *** Section 4 writes a real Draco GLB with dracoEncode.mjs, then decodes it through this tree's OWN shipped path -- three's real GLTFLoader plus the vendored DRACOLoader.js, in an actual headless-Chromium page (tools/ship/webgpuHarness.mjs's runInEngineOrigin, extended with a manually-injected <script type=\"importmap\"> since GLTFLoader.js and DRACOLoader.js both bare-import 'three' and runInEngineOrigin's own minimal page shell carries no import map the way pages like universal-viewer.html do). The decoded mesh is compared to the input by GEOMETRIC equality rather than array equality -- Draco's edgebreaker traversal renumbers vertices and reorders faces on purpose, measured directly on this exact round trip (input vertex order [0,1,2,3] came back as [3,2,1,0]) -- so each triangle is canonicalised as its three sorted (position,normal,uv) corners and the SORTED SET of triangles is compared, which is invariant to exactly the reordering Draco does and nothing else. Both triangles matched exactly.\n\nMEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, ON REAL GEOMETRY: a 6x6 grid of unit quads (144 unwelded corners, weldVertices.mjs's own \"where the saving actually comes from\" shape) welds to 49 vertices / 72 triangles and Draco-encodes to 182 bytes against 2,040 bytes of raw FLOAT position+normal plus uint32 indices -- 91.1% smaller, on exactly the quantised-integer-position geometry weldVertices.mjs's header predicted would be \"the big win\".\n\nPermanent gate: tools/ship/dracoEncode-selfcheck.mjs (6 sections -- the encoder module itself including the rejected-build regression note, attribute-id capture across every combination, GLB container conformance including the TEXCOORD_0/COLOR_0 key regression check, refusals, the real end-to-end decode, and the measured compression ratio). tools/ship/dracoWeld-selfcheck.mjs's own closing note is updated to point here rather than repeat these numbers a second place.\n\nSTILL NOT DONE: tools/export/voxelGlb.mjs's writeGlb does not gain a draco option directly -- kept as a separate async writer (tools/export/dracoEncode.mjs's writeDracoGlb) because Draco's module init is asynchronous and voxelGlb.mjs's own writeGlb (and weldVertices.mjs's weld()) are both deliberately synchronous; making writeGlb async would ripple through every existing caller for a feature most of them do not want. Also not done: texture compression (KTX2/Basis) -- unchanged from tools/ship/todo.mjs's own measured won't-do, since nothing about this round changed the tree's texture footprint; meshopt encoding (the ENCODE half -- decoding was already built) -- named in the original ask alongside Draco but not built this round, since Draco alone already answers weldVertices.mjs's own named want and a second encoder needs its own separate justification rather than riding along on this one; point-cloud encoding (EncodePointCloudToDracoBuffer exists in the vendored module, untouched); and pruning/dedup of unused materials, textures or duplicate data (this tree's own hand-written exporters do not accumulate that kind of cruft the way a general-purpose importer/re-exporter would, so the want this round's own research turned up was narrower than the original ask -- the vendored Kenney/Quaternius kit GLBs this tree reads, rather than writes, are the more plausible place such pruning would ever matter, and nothing measures that yet).",
